Personal Reflection on Early Stages of the Project Process

Project Progress Overview

The initial phases of this allergen-filtered recipe search project have progressed significantly beyond the original timeline expectations, with Weeks 1-2 Literature Review “draft” ready for submission completed and early implementation work from Weeks 7-10 already underway. 

Literature Review Development (Weeks 1-2)

The literature review phase revealed itself to be far more complex than initially anticipated. What began as a straightforward survey of web scraping techniques and allergen classification systems evolved into a critical examination of the ethical tensions inherent in building safety critical health applications using automated data collection. 
The review identified three fundamental gaps in existing literature: 
 -the treatment of accuracy as purely statistical rather than ethical.
 -the focus on protecting data sources rather than end users.
 -the absence of serious engagement with automated allergen detection systems in public health literature.

This critical perspective emerged from engaging with sources like Roither et al. (2022), having 90% accuracy rates which are respectable from a machine learning perspective, represent potentially catastrophic failure rates in health applications. The realisation that one incorrectly classified recipe in ten could prove fatal for someone with severe allergies fundamentally shifted the project's philosophical approach. Rather than simply building a functional tool, the project now explicitly grapples with whether such tools should exist at all, and if so, under what constraints.

The integration of Chaudhari et al. (2020) during the later review stages proved particularly valuable, as their multi stage architecture (scraping to cleaning to analysis) provided a robust technical framework while highlighting the challenge of data heterogeneity across recipe websites. This insight directly informed the decision to implement flexible scraping strategies with multiple fallback mechanisms rather than rigid, site-specific approaches.

Early Technical Implementation (Supposed to be Weeks 7-10)

A deviation to the original timeline, technical development began concurrently with literature review completion. Building safety features after building core functionality would have been philosophically inconsistent with the project's central argument that such systems must be "designed from the ground up to acknowledge their limitations loudly and persistently."

The scraper implementation incorporates ethical web scraping practices from the outset; robots.txt compliance, rate limiting, clear user-agent identification, and comprehensive error handling. The allergen detection system implements a multi layered approach (lexical, compound ingredient, hidden source, and specific type detection) with confidence scoring for every detection. This technical choice directly addresses the literature review's critique that existing systems treat all detections as equally dependable.

Additions to the project include an unavoidable critical safety banner, mandatory user verification checkboxes, and repeated disclaimers throughout the interface.

Adjustments and Justifications

1.	Enhanced Safety Requirements
 The original proposal specified 95% accuracy as acceptable. The literature review revealed this threshold to be inadequate for safety-critical applications. The revised approach implements conservative filtering (prioritising false positives over false negatives) and requires explicit user acknowledgment of system limitations before viewing any recipe. This change is justified by the ethical need that convenience must never supersede safety in health applications.

2.	Multi-Stage Architecture: 
Inspired by Chaudhari et al. (2020), the scraper now implements a three-stage pipeline with quality assessment at each stage. This represents a departure from the original plan's simpler scraping approach but provides essential robustness for handling diverse website structures and ensuring data reliability.

3. Confidence Scoring System: Not specified in the original proposal, confidence scoring emerged as essential during literature review. Every allergen detection now includes HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW confidence indicators, detection methods, and matched keywords. This transparency allows users to make informed risk assessments rather than blindly trusting automated classifications.

4. Accelerated Timeline: The overlap between literature review and technical implementation, while unplanned, has proven beneficial. Early technical work informed theoretical understanding, while ethical insights shaped technical decisions in real-time. This approach better serves the goal of demonstrating responsible health technology development.

Challenges and Adaptations

The primary challenge has been balancing technical sophistication with ethical responsibility. This philosophical stance has led to interface designs that prioritise safety warnings over aesthetic appeal, a deliberate choice that may affect usability metrics but better serves the vulnerable populations this tool aims to protect.


Looking Forward

The project now enters the technical feasibility study phase (Weeks 3-4) with substantial groundwork already completed. The literature review's ethical framework will guide all subsequent development decisions, ensuring that safety considerations remain paramount. The enhanced scraper architecture, informed by both academic literature and early implementation experience, provides a solid foundation for the allergen detection and classification system (Weeks 9-10).

The progress positions the project well for the evaluation phase (Week 13), where the question, whether a system designed to acknowledge its limitations can still provide value while minimising harm will be empirically tested. The comprehensive documentation created during these early stages will facilitate both the dissertation writing process and future research in this critical domain.















 
 
Literature Review: Web Scraping Ethics and Digital Food Allergen Management Systems

Introduction

When a 15-year-old girl died in 2016 after eating a Pret a Manger baguette containing sesame seeds not listed on the label, the tragedy sparked not only legal reform but a broader recognition that allergen information systems are failing vulnerable populations. The subsequent introduction of Natasha's Law in 2021 mandated clearer allergen labelling, yet this regulatory response addresses only packaged foods, leaving a vast digital landscape of online recipes largely unregulated and potentially dangerous for the millions managing food allergies. This gap between regulatory protection and digital reality makes up the central arguement explored in this review.

The question is not whether technology can help people with food allergies navigate online recipes more safely and several researchers have demonstrated technical feasibility. Rather, the more pressing question is whether such technology should be built using web scraping, and if so, under what ethical constraints. Kelly (2024) documents the troubling 20-year rise of food allergies in England, with hospital admissions for anaphylaxis climbing steadily. Yet the academic response has been curiously divided. Computer scientists develop increasingly sophisticated scraping and classification algorithms with minimal engagement with health ethics, while public health researchers discuss allergen management mostly without considering the technical problems and pitfalls of automated data collection. This review argues that neither approach is sufficient. What we need, and what the literature lacks, is a framework that treats web scraping for health applications as fundamentally different from scraping for commercial or general research purposes, demanding higher ethical standards precisely because the stakes involve human safety rather than mere data accuracy.

The Public Health Context of Food Allergies

Rising Prevalence and Clinical Significance

Kelly (2024) presents what should be alarming data from a 20-year English cohort study, yet the findings arrive with a curious lack of urgency in their implications for digital health interventions. Hospital admissions for anaphylaxis have risen, with food allergies driving much of this increase, particularly among children and young adults. What Kelly does not address is the disconnect between that allergic reactions occurring increasingly at home, using recipes found online and where regulatory attention focuses primarily on commercial food establishments and packaged products. This oversight matters because it reveals a blind spot in public health thinking, the assumption that better labelling alone will solve the problem, when in fact the large a amount of unregulated recipe content online may be creating new risks faster than traditional regulatory mechanisms can address them.

The clinical significance of food allergies extends beyond immediate allergic reactions to encompass broader impacts on quality of life, psychological wellbeing, and social participation. Kelly (2024) notes that individuals with food allergies face constant vigilance in food selection, often experiencing anxiety about accidental exposure and social isolation due to dietary restrictions. The study emphasises that effective allergen management requires not only medical interventions but also practical tools that enable individuals to navigate daily food choices safely and confidently. This context establishes the rationale for developing digital tools that can assist in identifying safe food options, specifically in the domain of recipe selection where ingredient information may be complex or incomplete.

Regulatory Framework for Allergen Declaration

Anaphylaxis UK (2023) dutifully lists the 14 major allergens mandated for declaration under UK and EU law, but the organisation's guidance reveals an uncomfortable truth: these regulations were designed for a pre-digital food landscape. The list itself (cereals containing gluten, crustaceans, eggs, fish, peanuts, soybeans, milk, tree nuts, celery, mustard, sesame, sulphites, lupin, and molluscs) emerged from decades of clinical evidence and lobbying, yet it assumes a world where food information flows through regulated channels like product labels and restaurant menus. Online recipes exist in a regulatory grey zone, neither clearly covered by food information regulations nor entirely exempt from them. This ambiguity creates both the opportunity and the ethical dilemma for automated allergen detection systems, they could fill a genuine safety gap, but they might also create false confidence in unregulated information.

Anaphylaxis UK (2023) provides detailed information about each allergen group, including common sources, cross-reactivity patterns, and practical management strategies. This comprehensive framework establishes the knowledge base required for developing automated allergen detection systems. The organisation notes that allergen management is complicated by several factors including hidden allergens in processed foods, cross-contamination during food preparation, and variability in allergen labelling practices across different contexts. These challenges underscore the need for digital tools that can gather and analyse ingredient information from multiple sources, providing users with comprehensive allergen assessments that account for the complexity of real-world food environments.

The regulatory framework described by Anaphylaxis UK (2023) also establishes important considerations for the design of allergen filtered recipe systems. The organisation emphasises that allergen information must be accurate, up-to-date, and presented in a clear, accessible format. Furthermore, they note that individuals with food allergies require not only information about allergen presence but also guidance about safe alternatives and strategies for adapting recipes to accommodate dietary restrictions. These requirements suggest that effective allergen management tools must go beyond simple allergen detection to provide actionable information that supports safe and enjoyable food experiences.

Technical Approaches and Methodologies for Web Scraping

Khder (2021) offers a technical survey of web scraping methodologies, distinguishing between targeted scraping and broad crawling, but the review suffers from a problem endemic to computer science literature, it treats all scraping targets as morally equivalent. Whether one is scraping product prices, news articles, or recipe ingredients receives identical technical treatment, as though the ethical implications of automated data collection were invariant across domains. Scraping recipe data for allergen detection is not like scraping stock prices or weather data because errors in the former can hospitalise or kill people, while errors in the latter merely inconvenience them. Yet Khder's review, like much of the technical literature, proceeds as though reliability were purely a technical concern rather than an ethical one that might demand different approaches for different domains.

Khder (2021) categorises web scraping techniques into several approaches including HTML parsing, DOM parsing, and vertical aggregation. HTML parsing involves analysing the raw HTML code of web pages to extract desired information, while DOM parsing works with the Document Object Model representation of web pages, allowing for more sophisticated extraction based on page structure. Vertical aggregation focuses on collecting data from multiple sources within a specific domain, which aligns closely with the requirements of recipe aggregation systems. The author notes that each approach has distinct advantages and limitations, with the choice of technique depending on factors such as the structure of target websites, the complexity of desired data, and the scale of collection activities.

Sharma and Borkar (2024) extend this technical discussion by examining dynamic web scraping strategies, which are necessary for extracting data from websites that use JavaScript to generate content dynamically. The authors conduct a comparative analysis of different techniques for handling dynamic content, including browser automation, headless browsers, and API interception. They note that many modern recipe websites employ dynamic content loading to improve user experience, but this creates challenges for traditional scraping approaches that rely on static HTML analysis. The authors propose a framework for evaluating different dynamic scraping strategies based on factors including reliability, performance, and resource requirements.

The technical considerations discussed by Sharma and Borkar (2024) have important implications for allergen-filtered recipe systems. Dynamic content loading means that ingredient information may not be immediately available in the initial HTML response, requiring scrapers to execute JavaScript or wait for content to load before extraction can occur. This technical complexity increases the computational resources required for scraping and introduces additional points of potential failure. The authors emphasise that robust scraping systems must implement sophisticated error handling and retry mechanisms to ensure reliable data collection despite these technical challenges.


Several researchers have examined the specific challenges and opportunities of applying web scraping to recipe data. Suwalka et al. (2023) present Food Genie, a recipe search algorithm using web scraping to gather recipes from multiple online sources. The authors describe a system that scrapes recipe websites, extracts structured information about ingredients and cooking instructions, and provides search functionality based on available ingredients. Their work demonstrates the feasibility of automated recipe aggregation while highlighting several technical challenges including variability in formatting across websites, ambiguity in ingredient descriptions, and the need for natural language processing to interpret cooking instructions.

The Food Genie system described by Suwalka et al. (2023) implements several technical innovations relevant to allergen-filtered recipe search. The authors develop algorithms for normalising ingredient names across different websites, recognising that the same ingredient may be described in multiple ways. They also implement techniques for extracting structured data from unstructured text, using pattern matching and machine learning approaches to identify ingredient quantities, preparation methods, and cooking times. These technical methods are essential for allergen detection, as accurate identification of allergens requires precise understanding of ingredient composition rather than simple keyword matching.

An earlier work examining ingredient and recipe algorithms using web mining and web scraping for Smart Chef systems demonstrates the evolution of this research area. The CHAUDHARI ET AL., 2020 publication explores how web scraping can be combined with data mining techniques to create intelligent recipe recommendation systems. The authors examine challenges in extracting recipe data from diverse web sources, noting that different websites employ varying structures and formats for presenting recipe information. They propose a multi-stage approach involving web scraping for data collection, data cleaning to standardise extracted information, and machine learning for recipe analysis and recommendation. This work establishes important precedents for the technical architecture of allergen-filtered recipe systems.

Automated Allergen Identification Approaches
Roither, Kurz, and Sonnleitner (2022) deserve credit for attempting serious allergen classification in recipes, but their Applied Sciences paper inadvertently exposes a troubling gap between technical achievement and clinical responsibility. They report accuracy rates exceeding 90 percent for most allergen categories, presenting this as a success. From a machine learning perspective, 90 percent accuracy is indeed respectable. From a health perspective, it is potentially catastrophic. Consider what a 10 percent error rate means in practice: if someone with a severe peanut allergy uses a system with 90 percent accuracy to filter 100 recipes, statistically they will encounter 10 recipes incorrectly classified as safe. One of those errors could prove fatal. The authors acknowledge this limitation in a single paragraph before moving on, but this deserves to be a central concern not a footnote. No amount of technical sophistication can eliminate the fundamental problem that automated systems will make mistakes, and in health applications, mistakes have consequences that accuracy metrics alone cannot capture.

The system described by Roither et al. (2022) employs a multi-layered approach to allergen detection. The first layer involves lexical analysis, identifying allergen-related terms in ingredient lists through pattern matching against comprehensive allergen dictionaries. The second layer applies semantic analysis, using word embeddings and contextual understanding to identify allergens even when they are described using non-standard terminology or appear as components of compound ingredients. The third layer implements rule-based reasoning, applying domain knowledge about ingredient relationships and food preparation to infer allergen presence even when not explicitly stated. This sophisticated approach addresses the complexity of real-world recipe data, where allergens may be present but not immediately obvious from ingredient descriptions.

Roither et al. (2022) also examine the challenge of allergen classification accuracy, conducting extensive validation against manually annotated recipe datasets. Their results indicate that automated allergen detection can achieve accuracy rates exceeding 90 percent for most allergen categories, though performance varies depending on the specific allergen and the quality of ingredient descriptions. The authors note that certain allergens, particularly those that may be present as minor ingredients or processing aids, are more difficult to detect automatically. They emphasise the importance of conservative classification approaches that take the safer option on the side of flagging potential allergens rather than missing them, given the serious health consequences of false negatives in allergen detection.

Recent Advances in Allergen Detection Systems

More recent work by Sharma et al. (2025) examines food allergen detection and recommendation systems, presenting advances in both detection algorithms and user interface design. Sharma, P. et al. (2025) their work explores how allergen detection systems can be integrated with recipe recommendation engines to provide personalised suggestions based on individual dietary restrictions. The authors develop algorithms that not only identify allergens in recipes but also suggest modifications or alternatives that would make recipes safe for individuals with specific allergies. This approach recognises that effective allergen management involves not just avoiding problematic foods but also finding satisfying alternatives.

Sharma et al. (2025) introduce several technical innovations in their allergen detection system. They implement a hierarchical allergen taxonomy that captures relationships between different allergens and ingredient categories, enabling more sophisticated reasoning about allergen presence. For example, their system understands that items labelled as dairy products likely contain milk allergens, even if milk is not explicitly listed as an ingredient. They also develop confidence scoring mechanisms that indicate the reliability of allergen classifications, allowing users to make informed decisions about whether to trust automated assessments or seek additional verification.

The recommendation component of the system described by Sharma et al. (2025) represents an important advance beyond simple allergen filtering. Rather than merely excluding recipes that contain problematic allergens, their system actively suggests recipes that meet dietary requirements while also considering factors such as nutritional balance, ingredient availability, and user preferences. This holistic approach to allergen management recognises that individuals with food allergies need not just safety information but also positive guidance about what they can eat. The authors validate their recommendation algorithms through user studies, demonstrating that personalised, allergen-aware recommendations significantly improve user satisfaction and dietary diversity.


Trzmielewski (2024) contributes a theoretical perspective on allergen management through the development of an allergy ontology. The author argues that effective allergen management systems require formal knowledge representation frameworks that capture the complex relationships between allergens, ingredients, foods, and allergic reactions. An ontology based approach enables more sophisticated reasoning about allergen presence and risk, moving beyond simple keyword matching to incorporate domain knowledge about food composition, cross-reactivity, and individual sensitivity patterns.

The utility-based contextual approach proposed by Trzmielewski (2024) recognises that allergen management is inherently contextual, depending on factors such as the severity of individual allergies, the likelihood of cross contamination in different food preparation, and the availability of allergen information. The author develops a formal ontology that represents these contextual factors, enabling allergen management systems to provide personalised risk assessments rather than one size fits all classifications. This approach acknowledges that individuals with food allergies have varying risk tolerances and information needs, requiring flexible systems that can adapt to different use cases.

Trzmielewski (2024) also examines the challenge of maintaining and updating allergen knowledge bases as scientific understanding evolves and new allergens are identified. The ontological framework provides mechanisms for incorporating new knowledge without requiring fundamental restructuring of the system, supporting long term sustainability of allergen management tools. The author emphasises that ontology-based approaches facilitate interoperability between different allergen management systems, enabling data sharing and collaborative development of allergen knowledge resources. This perspective suggests that effective allergen management requires not just individual applications but coordinated ecosystems of tools and data sources.





Ethical and Legal Frameworks for Web Scraping

Brown et al. (2024) have produced what is the most comprehensive framework for ethical web scraping, this reveals the limitations of applying general ethical principles to health-specific applications. Their framework addresses respect for data sources, privacy protection, legal compliance, and scientific integrity, all of which are important. It lacks any discussion of downstream harm to end users of scraped data. The framework is built around protecting websites and their users from scraping harms, but it does not adequately consider scenarios where scraped data, even if collected ethically, might harm people who rely on it. This is not a flaw of Brown et al.'s rather how web scraping ethics has developed, primarily in response to concerns about data theft and server overload, not about whether scraped information is fit for safety-critical purposes.

Brown et al. (2024) argue that web scraping exists stuck between technological capability and social responsibility, requiring researchers to navigate a complex terrain where traditional scraping ethics may not provide enough guidance. The authors identify several key ethical principles that should guide web scraping activities. First, they emphasise the principle of respect for persons, arguing that even publicly accessible data may carry privacy expectations and that scrapers should consider the potential impacts of data collection on individuals whose information appears online. 

The framework proposed by Brown et al. (2024) addresses the institutional and scientific ethics of web scraping. They note that researchers conducting web scraping must navigate institutional policies, including requirements for ethical review and data management. The authors argue that while traditional institutional review board frameworks may not directly apply to web scraping of public data, researchers should nonetheless engage with ethical review processes to ensure their activities align with institutional values and community standards. They emphasise the scientific side of ethical web scraping, noting that poor quality data collection can lead to flawed research conclusions, which itself represents an ethical failure when such research informs policy or practice.

Regulatory and Policy Frameworks

The Food Standards Agency (2022) provides important regulatory context through its web scraping policy, which establishes guidelines for scraping food-related information from government websites. The policy recognises the value of making food safety and allergen information widely accessible while also protecting the integrity of official data sources. The agency permits web scraping of publicly available information for legitimate purposes including research, public health applications, and consumer information services, provided that scraping activities do not interfere with normal website operations or violate terms of service.

The Food Standards Agency (2022) policy establishes several specific requirements for ethical web scraping of food information. Scrapers must identify themselves clearly through User-Agent strings, implement rate limiting to avoid overwhelming servers, respect robots.txt directives, and provide when using scraped data. The policy also addresses data quality concerns, noting that scrapers are responsible for ensuring that extracted information accurately reflects source data and that any processing or transformation is clearly documented. These requirements reflect broader principles of transparency and accountability for data sources that characterise ethical web scraping practices.

The regulatory framework established by the Food Standards Agency (2022) has particular relevance for allergen-filtered recipe systems, as such systems may scrape food-related information from both government and commercial sources. The policy suggests that regulatory bodies recognise the public value of tools that make allergen information more accessible, while also emphasising the need for responsible data collection practices. The agency's approach of permitting scraping while establishing clear guidelines provides a model for how web scraping can be accommodated within regulatory frameworks, balancing openness with protection of data integrity.


Food Data Scraper (no date) examines the role of scraping nutritional information from food websites from a commercial perspective, highlighting both the opportunities and challenges of automated nutritional data collection. It discusses how web scraping enables the gathering of nutritional information from diverse sources that create databases that can support applications including dietary planning, nutritional analysis, and allergen management. 
The commercial context introduces additional considerations beyond those present in academic research, including intellectual property concerns and competitive dynamics.

The commercial perspective provided by Food Data Scraper emphasises the importance of establishing sustainable relationships with data sources. While academic research may involve one-time or limited data collection, commercial applications often require ongoing scraping to maintain current information. This sustained relationship with data sources necessitates particularly careful attention to ethical considerations, as aggressive or disruptive scraping practices could damage relationships and result in loss of access.
They also discus technical challenges specific to nutritional data scraping, including variability in how nutritional information is presented across websites, the need for normalisation to enable comparison across sources, and the challenge of maintaining data as recipes and nutritional information are updated. These technical challenges have important implications for allergen filtered recipe systems, as allergen information must be not only accurate at the time of collection but also kept current as recipes or websites are changed. 




Technical and Ethical Frameworks
The technical literature demonstrates feasibility while the ethical literature establishes general principles, but neither adequately addresses whether automated allergen detection systems built on scraped data should exist at all, let alone how they should be designed. This is not a gap that can be filled by simply combining existing frameworks. Suwalka et al. (2023), Roither et al. (2022), and Sharma et al. (2025) show us that we can build these systems. Brown et al. (2024) and the Food Standards Agency (2022) tell us how to scrape responsibly. But none of them seriously challenge the possibility that a responsibly scraped, technically sophisticated allergen detection system might still be irresponsible to deploy if it creates false confidence in automated safety assessments.

This review argues for a more cautious position than the literature generally supports. Yes, such systems should be built, but only if they are designed from the ground up to acknowledge their limitations loudly and persistently. This means not just disclaimers buried in terms of service, but interface designs that make uncertainty visible, that default to caution rather than convenience. The technical capability exists, and the public health need is real, but the gap between what we can build and what we should build remains wider than the existing literature acknowledges.

The integration of these technical and ethical frameworks addresses a genuine public health need identified by Kelly (2024) and Anaphylaxis UK (2023). Rising food allergy prevalence, combined with the complexity of managing dietary restrictions in contemporary food environments, creates demand for tools that can help individuals identify safe food options. The vast amount of recipe data available online represents an underutilised resource that, if accessed responsibly through ethical web scraping practices, can significantly improve allergen management capabilities. The project proposed in this dissertation demonstrates how technical capability can be harnessed to serve public health goals while respecting the rights and interests of data sources.

The ontological perspective provided by Trzmielewski (2024) suggests that the proposed system incorporates these insights by implementing allergen detection algorithms that go beyond simple keyword matching to incorporate domain knowledge about ingredient relationships and allergen characteristics. This approach recognises that allergen management is a complex task requiring integration of multiple knowledge sources and reasoning strategies, rather than a simple filtering operation.

Limitations 
A critical aspect of justifying this project involves acknowledging its limitations and implementing safeguards to protect user safety. The work of Roither et al. (2022) demonstrates that while automated allergen detection can achieve high accuracy, it is not perfect. The proposed system addresses this limitation through several mechanisms. First, it implements conservative allergen flagging that prioritises false positives over false negatives, recognising that it is better to unnecessarily exclude a safe recipe than to incorrectly classify a recipe containing allergens as safe. Second, it provides clear disclaimers informing users that automated allergen detection cannot replace careful ingredient verification and consultation with healthcare providers.

The technical challenges identified by Sharma and Borkar (2024) regarding dynamic web scraping also inform the system design. The proposed system implements robust error handling and fallback mechanisms to ensure continued functionality even when scraping of sources fails. Mock data provides a safety net ensuring that users can access basic functionality even when live scraping is unavailable, while modular design facilitates updates to scraping logic as website structures change. These technical choices reflect a commitment to reliability and sustainability, recognising that allergen management tools must function consistently to be trustworthy.

The ethical framework proposed by Brown et al. (2024) emphasises the importance of transparency and accountability in web scraping projects. The proposed system implements these principles through clear documentation of data sources, transparent communication about allergen detection methodologies, and mechanisms for user feedback about accuracy and usability. This transparency serves multiple purposes, it enables users to make informed decisions about how much to rely on the system, it facilitates ongoing improvement through identification of errors or limitations, and it demonstrates good faith commitment to ethical data collection practices.






This project contributes to both theoretical understanding and practical application in several interconnected ways. It demonstrates how ethical frameworks for web scraping can be used in health related applications. 

Practically, the project creates a functional tool that addresses the public health need identified by Kelly (2024) while implementing the technical approaches demonstrated by Suwalka et al. (2023) and Roither et al. (2022). By combining recipe gathering through ethical web scraping with sophisticated allergen detection algorithms, the system provides capabilities that are not readily available through existing tools. 

The project also contributes to the growing body of work on digital allergen management, an area that Sharma et al. (2025) and Trzmielewski (2024) identify as requiring further development. By documenting the development process, including technical decisions, ethical considerations, and challenges encountered, this project provides a resource for other researchers and developers working in this domain. 


Conclusion

The literature examined here tells a story of technical progress outpacing ethical reflection. We know how to scrape recipe websites (Khder 2021) (Sharma and Borkar 2024), we know how to detect allergens in ingredient lists with reasonable accuracy (Roither et al. 2022), and we know the public health need is real and growing (Kelly 2024). What we do not yet know, and what the literature has not adequately addressed, is whether the inevitable errors in such systems are acceptable risks given the potential benefits.

The proposed project proceeds from a position of what might be called cautious optimism. Optimistic that technology can help people with food allergies navigate online recipes more safely, but cautious about the limitations and potential harms of automated systems. This stance differs from both the uncritical enthusiasm of much technical literature and the implicit scepticism of public health researchers who have largely ignored digital recipe platforms as sites of intervention. The project accepts that perfect allergen detection is impossible, that web scraping will sometimes fail, and that users will sometimes misunderstand or over rely on automated assessments. The question is whether a system designed with these limitations in mind, which makes uncertainty visible and demands active user engagement, can still provide value while minimising harm.

This review has identified three critical gaps in the existing literature that the proposed project must address. 
Firstly, the technical literature treats accuracy as a purely statistical concern rather than a threshold question about whether deployment is ethical. 
Secondly, the web scraping ethics in literature focuses heavily on protecting data sources rather than protecting end users of scraped data. 
Thirdly, the public health literature on allergen management has not engaged seriously with the possibilities and perils of automated systems built on scraped data. 
Filling these gaps requires not just technical competence and ethical scraping practices, but a fundamental rethinking of what responsible health technology looks like when perfect accuracy is unattainable, and the consequences of errors are potentially severe.






